export abstract anchor FactoryPlanning:
    orderSubmission:
        The engineer may discuss work with a chosen conversational agent or ask
        it to break down a feature. When the engineer instructs it to begin, that
        agent can submit an order summarizing its understanding to the factory.
        The conversational agent need not be the agent used for factory planning.
    conflictingOrders:
        When a new order conflicts with active work, delegate assessment of the
        conflict and present a concrete recommendation to the engineer,
        including work that would be replaced or reused. A new order does not
        silently cancel earlier intent. An explicit engineer instruction to
        replace the old approach authorizes proceeding under the existing
        supersession rules. Independent work may continue while clarification
        is pending.
    planningAndExecution:
        A smart, expensive agent plans work at a broad level sufficient for a
        mid-tier agent to elaborate. Mid-tier agents produce specific, detailed
        implementation instructions for cheap, fast implementation agents.
        This hierarchy guides delegation without prescribing a fixed pipeline.
    evolvingWorkflow:
        - An agent proposes the initial steps required to fulfill an order.
        - Steps are deliberately flexible rather than a fixed sequence of stage types.
        - Agents can revise steps as work progresses, within their authority.
        - The factory dispatches execution of the resulting steps.
        - A mid-tier agent proposes the initial workflow by default and can escalate difficult workflow decisions to a smarter agent.
    revisionAuthority:
        Agents can change work below their intelligence tier. Lower-tier agents
        can add steps to request help when required. The receiving agent decides
        whether to answer, escalate to a smarter agent, or disregard the request.
        Disregarding a request means replying to the requesting lower-tier
        agent that it does not need to ask for help with this issue and should
        handle it itself.
        Agents may propose changes to another agent's work at the same
        intelligence tier, but a smarter agent decides those changes. An agent
        may refine its own assigned work within its brief.
    stepContext:
        The delegating agent prepares a focused brief containing the step's
        objective, relevant requirements, specific instructions, expected output,
        and completion criteria. The brief links to the original order,
        applicable Piton specifications, and relevant prior results. The receiving
        agent starts with the brief and can consult broader context when needed
        or request help. The factory preserves briefs and results so handoffs do
        not depend on an agent remembering an earlier conversation.
    dependenciesAndScheduling:
        Agents explicitly declare and revise dependencies between steps. The
        factory enforces those dependencies and determines readiness without
        asking an agent to choose every next step. Independent steps may run
        concurrently once their prerequisites are satisfied, subject to
        configured concurrency limits and available account usage. The factory
        checks for missing prerequisites and circular dependencies before
        executing affected steps.
        Dependency readiness makes a step eligible to run; available agent
        concurrency and budget determine when it starts; build and test capacity
        determines when its jobs start.
    orderPriorities:
        When orders compete for constrained capacity, favor completing the
        highest-priority order sooner. Other orders may use capacity that the
        higher-priority order cannot use. At equal priorities, favor finishing
        work already underway to avoid accumulating half-finished results.
        Exact priority representation remains a project implementation choice.
    revisionsDuringExecution:
        The design should make revisions affecting running steps rare. When an
        authorized revision does affect running work, the factory marks that
        work as superseded, requests cancellation, and preserves its outputs for
        possible reuse. Replacement steps follow the revised plan. Superseded
        results do not automatically count toward completion.
    estimates:
        Agents estimate at the granularity that fits their level. Large tasks
        use t-shirt sizes. Mid-tier briefs use story points. Cheap
        implementation agents estimate in time. Agents also report progress,
        remaining work, and confidence through the defined operations.
        Estimates are advisory input, not commitments. The factory converts
        them into displayed completion ranges using plan progress and
        historical step durations or throughput, and reports unknown when
        evidence is insufficient. Estimates inform reporting; they do not gate
        dependency readiness, capacity allocation, or dispatch.
    dependencies:
        A dependency is a directed edge with a stable identifier, a dependent
        step, a prerequisite step, a kind, and an optional pinned revision.
        Edges are scoped to one order.
        A result edge requires the prerequisite to have an assessed result
        bound to an immutable revision; the result may still be unmerged. An
        integrated edge requires commit ancestry in the target branch or an
        explicit integration record. A pinned revision that is not an ancestor
        invalidates dependents.
        Only all-of requirements are supported. Optional and any-of
        dependencies are rejected with a diagnostic.
        A failed, cancelled, superseded, unresolved, or missing prerequisite
        blocks dependents until an authorized revision repairs or removes the
        edge.
        Duplicate edges collapse by dependent, prerequisite, and kind.
        Completed dependents record the prerequisite revisions consumed at
        start and completion. Invalidation propagates transitively for result
        edges; integrated edges invalidate when integration changes.
        Pre-completion invalidation revalidates or supersedes. Post-completion
        invalidation creates a successor order or incident because completed
        orders are immutable.
        Validation runs when a plan is proposed, when revisions merge, and when
        persisted plans load. Per-order plan versions use compare-and-swap and
        the merge owner serializes merges. Cycles or dangling edges pause the
        affected subtree, roll back to the last valid plan, and escalate. An
        invalid persisted plan pauses its order at startup.
        Writing steps declare touched paths or resources; an undeclared writing
        step is treated as touching the whole repository. Cross-order overlap
        uses a durable serialization lease with owner, fencing epoch, expiry,
        release on failure or cancellation, and restart recovery. The engineer
        configures overlap policy.
    overlapLeaseLifecycle:
        Overlap keys are repo-relative paths plus declared non-path resources
        such as ports, caches, databases, registries, and build outputs. A
        writing step that declares no non-path resource is denied dispatch.
        Path locking is policy-dependent because worktrees isolate paths.
        The key set is snapshotted at acquisition; adding a key releases and
        reacquires the full set in sorted order. Acquisition sorts keys to avoid
        deadlock. A stale epoch cannot perform factory-owned effects: branch
        publication, push, integration, delivery, cache or artifact publication.
        The factory exclusively owns the ref namespace, and integration reads
        only factory-recorded revisions.
        A worktree belongs to the step, is materialized per session, and is
        reused by retries. A retry after session loss starts from the step's
        recorded revision; replacement preserves first and creates a new
        worktree. An orphan never shares the owner's worktree.
        Expiry fences the writer but keeps a read-only preservation claim until
        preservation commits. Preservation copies or quarantines the tree, or
        confirms the process stopped and freezes it, then verifies the tree hash
        before removal. Intersecting writers wait only for preservation, not
        engineer disposition.
    concurrentPlanRevisions:
        Treat conflicting concurrent revisions to a plan as a merge conflict.
        Identify the steps directly changed and every step whose work depends
        on their output. Pause dispatch in that affected subtree and keep
        unrelated work moving. Preserve both revisions and their affected
        steps. Dispatch another authorized agent to merge them into one
        coherent plan, subject to the established revision authority. Where
        the changes are compatible, keep both. Where they disagree about
        implementation, the resolving agent decides; where the disagreement is
        about what the engineer wants, it escalates. Revalidate paused steps
        against the merged plan, revise or supersede those that changed, resume
        those still valid, and preserve earlier results for reuse. Dispatch
        resumes only after the merged plan passes the normal checks. If no
        agent with authority can resolve the conflict, it waits or escalates.
